= The Longshoremen

The union breaking laws and fragmentation among hundreds of employers mean that the longshoremen only have an informal meeting place: the baths named Supa Rando Tobishima.  Many longshoremen and the retired elders who serve as leaders frequent the sprawling bathhouse which was built back in the Golden Age of traffic long before the asteroid mining era bust.  

The bathhouse is extremely odd, its central space is a meticulously and faithfully reconstructed Roman bathhouse from the reign of emperor Hadrian (who is among the gods whose statues adorn the place).  But the place operates on centuries old Japanese customs, imitation furūtsu gyūnyū is all but mandatory (kōhī gyūnyū is nowhere to be found for some reason), Aikido classes are held in a space labeled "palaestra" and there is a strict bar on tattoos with no exceptions.  A grand lenticular mural shows Mount Fuji from some angles and Mount Vesuvius from others.  Every five years when the station passes Luna there is a celebration held at the garden of Minerva with genuine furūtsu gyūnyū made from imported cow's milk.  These traditions apparently date back to an ancient architect named Yoshida but he attributed his inspiration to a man he just called "Senpai" who is not recorded anywhere but Yoshida's accounts.

The bathhouse is open to all comers but actually getting invited into the conversations in the hypocaust where they talk business requires the longshoremen like you.  Those welcomed into the Ganban'yoku can be part of the discussion of where the longshoremen should put their efforts.

